---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site.ts';

const title = `Privacy — what ${SITE.name} keeps`;
const description = `What ${SITE.name} stores when you share a wish or light a star, and how that information is used.`;
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <Breadcrumbs trail={[{ name: 'Home', path: '/' }, { name: 'Privacy' }]} />
  <div class="page-head">
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <p class="lede">Short, because the site is short on excuses for collecting things.</p>
  </div>

  <article class="prose">
    <h2>What a submission stores</h2>
    <p>
      When you use <a href="/submit">the wish form</a>, four things are written to a single database table: the product name,
      the canonical URL, the category you chose, and the free-text description you wrote. Your email address is stored next to
      them for one purpose — sending the blessing and the card — and it is never rendered on any public page.
    </p>
    <p>
      We also store a keyed hash of the submitting IP address to limit rapid repeat submissions. It is a throttle, not a
      security control, and it is deleted along with the rest of the row.
    </p>

    <h2>What is published</h2>
    <p>
      Approved entries become a public page with the product name, its link, our description of it, the dates we indexed and
      last checked it, and the blessing. That is all a reviewer writes down, so that is all that appears.
    </p>

    <h2>Community stars</h2>
    <p>
      If you light a star, your browser keeps a random identifier and a record of which project you chose, so it can remember
      your choice. We store a one-way hash of that identifier alongside the project and vote date. It is not linked to your
      submission email or account, and you can clear it from your browser at any time.
    </p>

    <h2>Tracking, cookies and analytics</h2>
    <p>
      When a GA4 measurement ID is configured, Google Analytics runs by default to measure site visits and page activity.
      We do not send account fields, form contents or project prompts to Google Analytics. Analytics is configured with IP
      anonymization and advertising signals disabled; the site does not use advertising tags or cross-site ad tracking.
      Authentication uses a necessary, HttpOnly session cookie after you sign in.
    </p>

    <h2>Accounts</h2>
    <p>
      Email accounts store your email address and a salted password hash. Email verification links are single-use and expire
      after one day. Sign-in sessions are stored as one-way token hashes in D1 and use a necessary, HttpOnly, Secure cookie.
      Google sign-in stores the Google account identifier, verified email and display name needed to maintain your account.
      You can request account removal at <a href={'mailto:' + SITE.email}>{SITE.email}</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Retention and deletion</h2>
    <p>
      Pending submissions are cleared automatically after the review period. Published entries keep the blessing record but not the
      email: once the notification is confirmed delivered, the address is dropped from the row and only a delivery date
      remains. Ask for removal at <a href={'mailto:' + SITE.email}>{SITE.email}</a> and the entry comes down in the next
      review round; ask twice and the domain is blocked from resubmission.
    </p>

    <h2>Where it lives</h2>
    <p>
      Pages are served through Cloudflare. Submissions, accounts and sessions are stored in Cloudflare D1, and verification
      messages are sent through Cloudflare Email Service. Google sign-in uses a signed identity token that WishMeteor verifies;
      when GA4 is configured, page activity is sent to Google Analytics. Processing location and retention follow each provider's
      service settings and policies. The site is operated from the address on the <a href="/about">about page</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
